WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.850
Hi! The ferns in the garden never have flowers. So how do they reproduce? Today we'll learn about spores, and how to do a fair experiment.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.898
Ferns and mosses have no flowers and no seeds. They reproduce by spores.

3
00:00:18.048 --> 00:00:24.498
Turn over a fern leaf. The brown dots underneath are spore cases, and they hold the spores.

4
00:00:24.648 --> 00:00:31.578
Spores are very tiny and light. One plant makes huge numbers of them, and the wind spreads them.

5
00:00:31.728 --> 00:00:36.426
When spores land in a damp, shady place, they grow into new plants.

6
00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:44.634
Mushrooms also reproduce by spores, but remember: mushrooms are fungi, not plants.

7
00:00:44.784 --> 00:00:48.906
Seeds are made by flowering plants after fertilisation.

8
00:00:49.056 --> 00:00:56.490
A seed has a seed coat to protect it, and stored food, like the seed leaves, to help the young plant start growing.

9
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.026
Spores are much smaller than seeds, and there are far more of them.

10
00:01:01.176 --> 00:01:07.650
But both spores and seeds can grow into new plants, so the plant can carry on.

11
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.626
How do scientists find out if seeds need water to germinate? They do a fair test.

12
00:01:13.776 --> 00:01:23.178
In a fair test, we change only one thing. Here we change only the water: dish A has wet cotton wool, dish B has dry cotton wool.

13
00:01:23.328 --> 00:01:30.234
Everything else stays the same: the type and number of seeds, the temperature, the air and the size of the dish.

14
00:01:30.384 --> 00:01:36.162
Then we measure one thing: after a few days, how many seeds in each dish have germinated?

15
00:01:36.312 --> 00:01:42.978
If seeds in A germinate and seeds in B don't, we know that germination needs water.

16
00:01:43.128 --> 00:01:51.522
Watch out! If dish A has wet cotton wool in a cupboard, and dish B has dry cotton wool by the window, the test is not fair.

17
00:01:51.672 --> 00:01:58.122
Because both water and light were changed, we can't tell if the result was caused by the water or the light.

18
00:01:58.272 --> 00:02:04.458
In a fair test, change only one thing and keep everything else the same.

19
00:02:04.608 --> 00:02:13.122
Your turn! Where can you find a fern's spore cases? Pause and think.

20
00:02:13.272 --> 00:02:20.682
On the underside of its leaves. They are the little brown dots. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:20.832 --> 00:02:31.410
Another one! You want to know if temperature affects germination. What should you change? Pause and think.

22
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:40.434
Change only the temperature. Keep the water, air, and the type and number of seeds the same. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:40.584 --> 00:02:51.186
Last one! Mushrooms reproduce by spores. So is a mushroom a plant? Pause and think.

24
00:02:51.336 --> 00:02:59.754
No, a mushroom is a fungus. Not everything that makes spores is a plant. Did you get it right?

25
00:02:59.904 --> 00:03:14.658
Remember: ferns and mosses reproduce by spores, and flowering plants reproduce by seeds. Spores are tiny, light and very many; seeds have a seed coat and stored food. In a fair test, change only one thing.

26
00:03:14.808 --> 00:03:19.434
Go and look under a fern leaf in your garden!

